at is $(f+g)'(x)$ when $f$ and $g$ are differentiable?","options":["$f'(x)g'(x)$","$f'(x)+g'(x)$","$f(x)+g'(x)$","$f'(x)-g(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"085ca4390a38611074154fcd"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using the constant multiple rule, what is $(cf)'(x)$ when $c$ is real and $f$ is differentiable?","options":["$c f'(x)$","$c+f'(x)$","$f'(cx)$","$c^2 f(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ed6319f87618984e58fdcf9"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which formula is the product rule?","options":["$(fg)'(x) = f'(x)g'(x)$","$(fg)'(x) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x)$","$(fg)'(x) = f(x)+g(x)$","$(fg)'(x) = f'(x)-g'(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"99a54c64d576e32c9114241d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition is needed for the reciprocal rule for $\\frac{1}{g}$?","options":["$g$ is constant equal to $0$.","$g$ is not defined on its domain.","$g$ is uncountable.","$g$ is differentiable and nonzero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3aa53a0faf7b4069641ce1a8"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $(x^2+3x)'$?","options":["$x^2+3$","$2x$","$3x^2$","$2x+3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"472ea0bf388b0c6397616f34"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $(x^2\\sin x)'$?","options":["$2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x$","$2x\\cos x+x^2\\sin x$","$x^2\\cos x$","$2x\\sin x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdee6242da55e541807381d8"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly warns about the quotient rule?","options":["The denominator function $g$ must be zero.","The numerator function $f$ must be nonzero.","The denominator function $g$ must be nonzero.","The quotient rule has no assumptions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"783c9bb5f618b592a14845c4"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the product rule not simply $(fg)' = f'g'$?","options":["Only the derivative of the first factor contributes.","Only the derivative of the second factor contributes.","Both factors contribute: $(fg)'(x) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x)$.","The derivative is $f'(x)g'(x)$ when both factors are nonconstant."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3626755d16bcbf2b19327acb"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x) = x$ and $g(x) = x+1$. Where $g$ is nonzero, what is $\\ \\left(\\frac{f}{g}\\right)'(x)$ by the quotient rule?","options":["$\\frac{x}{(x+1)^2}$","$\\frac{2x+1}{(x+1)^2}$","$\\frac{1}{(x+1)^2}$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdba92c40fd37d5a6900de3b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiation_rules_132a9287_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $g$ be differentiable and nonzero. Which formula is correct for $\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{g}\\right)'(x)$?","options":["$\\frac{g'(x)}{g(x)^2}$","$-g'(x)g(x)^2$","$\\frac{1}{g'(x)}$","$-\\frac{g'(x)}{g(x)^2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9b07bef2447867948f57227"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the difference rule say for differentiable $f$ and $g$?","options":["$(f-g)'(x)=f'(x)g'(x)$","$(f-g)'(x)=f(x)-g'(x)$","$(f-g)'(x)=0$","$(f-g)'(x)=f'(x)-g'(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cd5cbff7c3d8d8ea62263df1"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which rule requires the denominator function to be nonzero?","options":["The constant function rule.","The identity function rule.","The quotient rule.","The sum rule only."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eb4a8292be9a578725b4865e"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the derivative of $1/g$ when $g$ is differentiable and nonzero?","options":["$g'(x)/g(x)^2$","$-g'(x)/g(x)^2$","$1/g'(x)$","$-g(x)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"315e5606222efa0fce129979"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using differentiation rules, what is $(x^2-3x)'$?","options":["$2x+3$","$x^2-3$","$2x-3$","$-3x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"390b6680593ca43eca54b84b"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $c=5$, what is $(5f)'(x)$ for differentiable $f$?","options":["$f'(5x)$","$5f'(x)$","$5+f'(x)$","$25f(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0c145d035027bec59f3801ee"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=x^2$. What is $(fg)'(x)$ by the product rule?","options":["$2x$","$x^3$","$3x^2$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af20c69c59dbf3fee98f7417"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What do differentiation rules preserve under algebraic operations?","options":["Discontinuity only.","Uncountability.","Empty domains.","Differentiability."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e0d8b8885b1576b1f2edec5b"},{"id":"expansion_pack_016_differentiation_rules_new_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $h(x)=\\frac{x^2+1}{x}$ where $x\\neq0$, what is $h'(x)$ by the quotient rule?","options":["$\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2}$","$\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}$","$2x+1$","$\\frac{1}{x^2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0ff1a23f45c340ca720b3391"}]}}